Pairs of questions, and how similar their embeddings are.

                                                        similarity
  What was revenue in 2024 Q4?                               0.946
  What was gross profit in 2024 Q3?                          0.735
  Which region was weakest in 2024 Q3?                       0.920
  What is the price cap under MSC-2024-118?                  0.945
  How many units were shipped in 2025 Q1?                    0.753
  What was Midwest revenue in 2025 Q3?                       0.967

  what was the revenue for 2024 q3                           0.898
  In 2024 Q3, which region performed worst?                  0.843
  Under MSC-2022-100, how long do we have to pay?            0.781

  must NOT share an answer: 0.735 to 0.967
  should share an answer:   0.781 to 0.898

  the two ranges overlap: no threshold separates them
  the best possible cut is 0.967, and it still gets 3 of 9 pairs wrong
    (0 dangerous pairs served from cache, 3 safe pairs missed)

That is the failure mode, and it is not a tuning problem.

'Revenue in 2024 Q3' and 'revenue in 2024 Q4' are one character apart and mean
entirely different things. An embedding is a measure of *aboutness*, and two
questions about the same thing with different answers are exactly what it is
built to score as similar.

So a semantic cache does not have a safe threshold. It has a threshold you
have chosen a wrongness rate for, and the honest version of shipping one is:

  measure the rate on your own question pairs, not on a benchmark
  never cache anything containing a figure, a date or an identifier
  key on the *retrieved documents* rather than the question, so two questions
    only share an answer when they share their evidence
  and expire it fast, because a wrong cached answer is wrong every time

An exact cache has none of these problems, hits less often, and is where to
start. §28.5 measures what it is worth on a mix of repeated questions.
